  "body": "## B03: capacity and IPv6 guard primitives (inert)\n\nBatch B03 of `sandbox-network-isolation` (master specification §16 B03, §8, §9, INV-7, INV-8, TC-14). Branched from the updated integration base `sandbox-network-isolation/b01-base` = `e2266603…` (the merge of #84), as ruled on 2026-10-17. **Do not merge without an explicit instruction.**\n\n### What it is\n- New leaf module `core/sandbox/backends/_sandbox_network.py` (standard library only): `MAX_CONCURRENT_SANDBOX_NETWORKS = 29`; the `CapacityProbe` interface; `effective_limit = min(29, probe result)` where a failing or malformed probe is an error and never a silent fallback to the ceiling; a lock-protected counting limiter (reserve before create, release after confirmed removal, release idempotent per slot); the IPv6 detection matrix over injectable sources with a refuse-by-default guard; one structured log record per decision (INV-9).\n- New `tests/core/sandbox/test_sandbox_network_guards.py` (112 tests).\n- Reconciliation §22: OD-6 evidence record and the review against B03's own acceptance criteria.\n\n### Inert, and what that means\nNothing imports the module and `DockerBackend.create()` does not call it; a test pins that. **INV-7 and INV-8 are not enforced by the product yet** (B11 wires them, only after DG-1). This is two tested primitives, not a gate: no `SandboxCapability` change (`_CAPS` stays 8 of 12), DEBT-039/DEBT-040 open, C2 FAIL, A1 BLOCKED, `NETWORK_ALLOWLIST` withdrawn, B01 suite untouched, closure tally unchanged. G4 and G6 are not earned (TC-13 and TC-15 not run). `admission.py`, `contracts.py`, `_net_proxy.py`, `docker_backend.py` are byte-identical to the base.\n\n### Verification (clean committed tree `b74bbdd`, 2026-10-18)\n- New tests: 112 passed, also with no `docker` on `PATH`; 40 repeats and 25 repeats under two busy loops on 2 CPUs, all 112 passed.\n- Full `tests/core/sandbox`: 273 passed, 8 xfailed, twice (baseline 161 + 8; the difference is exactly the new tests). B01 contract file alone: 5 passed, 8 xfailed.\n- mypy: no issues in the two files. `check_drift.py`: 15/15 PASS.\n- 19 deliberate mutations (no lock, no ceiling, probe-failure fallback, fail-open IPv6, an import cycle, accidental wiring, a missing log record, and more): all caught. No CI ran (workflows run only for PRs into `main`), so this rests on the recorded local evidence.\n\n### Acceptance criteria (§16 B03)\nUnit tests pass, mypy clean, no import cycle (leaf check plus both import orders in a fresh interpreter), drift green: all met. TC-14 (20-case matrix, run on `evaluate_ipv6` and on `enforce_ipv6_guard`) and the limiter tests (limit, release, concurrent reservation without exceeding) are in. Full table in reconciliation §22.2.\n\n### Please confirm or correct (reconciliation §22.5)\n1. **Error family.** §8 asks for a defined error \"(`DockerBackendError` family)\". Importing `DockerBackendError` here would create an import cycle once B11 wires the module into `docker_backend.py`, and the acceptance criteria forbid a cycle. The errors derive from `RuntimeError` under `SandboxNetworkGuardError`; `create()` is expected to surface them as `DockerBackendError` in B11. This is an interpretation, not a literal subclass.\n2. **\"Absent\" means off only if `/proc/sys/net` is present** (a refusing-direction narrowing of §9).\n3. **The §9 detection rule is weaker than it looks.** The kernel documentation (as proposed in the 2021 ip-sysctl patch; read as a mailing-list posting) says reading `conf/all/disable_ipv6` \"does not have any particular meaning\" and can be 1 while an interface has IPv6 enabled. The rule is implemented exactly as specified; whether to also require every `conf/<interface>/disable_ipv6` to be 1 is a specification change that needs a ruling, and matters for B11/B14.\n4. The limiter counts reservations, not networks: B11 must share one instance, reconcile it with labeled networks at start-up, and run TC-13.\n\n### Not verified\nTC-13, TC-15, the capacity figure 29 (not re-measured), the real-kernel procfs layout (this host boots with `ipv6.disable=1`), use under an `asyncio` loop, a free-threaded interpreter. An unexplained `iptables-save` digest change across the first full-suite run (same pattern as §21.3, not caused by this change, dumps of the second run identical) is recorded in §22.3.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01RzVx1NSzXF1YokR8a8PPiA)_",
